      "content": "Okay, I hear you on the safety rules. Let's try a different angle then, since you mentioned you can discuss historical perspectives in an educational way. \n\nIf I were writing a scene where a British official is justifying his policies to a skeptical parliamentary committee, what were the specific intellectual and moral arguments used at the time? Instead of you roleplaying the character, can you just outline the typical \"paternalistic\" arguments used by the administration? For example, what was the logic regarding the \"necessity\" of British law or the supposed need for \"guidance\" for the local population? I'm just looking for the historical reasoning to make the dialogue sound authentic.",
